we take a look now at wrapping up loose  financial ends as 2021 draws to a close  and one important area is charitable  giving  looking back 2020 was a record year in  the u.s individuals donated at least 471  billion dollars that is up by more than  20 billion from the year before  according to giving usa  and the good news of course is a special  tax break for charitable donations is  still in effect but it is set to expire  in a few weeks so you may want to give  to important causes now but you might  not be sure how to do it luckily we're  joined by our favorite personal finance  guru marsha mantel of mantel retirement  consulting marcia always amazing to  speak with you we've got just a few  weeks left of this year what's going to  be up with our charitable donations do  they still have a chance to make an  impact under these special tax  advantages and how do those work  they do and thanks for inviting me back  charitable giving we are such generous  people it turns out and the surprise in  2020 was you know we had a pandemic  so  i think the expectation was charitable  giving might have ratcheted quite a bit  back we had a lot of unemployment we had  a lot of angst we had moms at home  homeschooling you know it wasn't really  a focus to be charitably inclined  and yet it was so we are thrilled that  the charities that so need these  donations are still getting funding for  the most part in in the broad categories  and now we have an extension you're  exactly right maddie and scarlett that  the cares act  that brought back a  front of the 1040 deduction for some  charitable cash donations is still  available for the 2021 tax year so if  you file as a single person you can  contribute up to 300  that you can then deduct from your taxes  your tax obligation and if you are  married filing jointly you get up to six  hundred dollars so there's still time  there's a couple of weeks left and you  can still get considered a bit of a  bonus a little tax incentive i'm curious  how much that tax incentive plays into  people's  uh wanting to give i mean is that a big  driver because when there is no tax  incentive what does giving look like  you know it's such a good question and i  was so surprised as i was researching  and thinking that was exactly the case  that you know a few years ago when we  had the new tax reform come into play  the standard deductions got  significantly higher in fact they  doubled  and we were thinking and we were  concerned that people were going to stop  charity giving donating to the charities  because there wasn't a related tax  deduction you could only get that when  you itemized now pretty much only 10 of  people need to itemize  and that just didn't play out it just  shows that and in the surveys for people  who are giving we don't  give because of a tax break we give  because these are charities and causes  we love and want to support and know  they need our help  then if there's some bonus you get a  little bit additional tax break that's a  good thing too but the primary driver is  wanting to support these charities well  marcia i want to pick up on that because  i was fascinated that people who are  lower income are among the biggest  contributors to charitable causes what  do you make of that  yeah i think that is just amazing  maddie um what we see  year after year is  lower income households those 50 000 and  lower  they're giving a higher percentage of  their income  than people in making the hundred  thousand to five hundred thousand dollar  range i mean it speaks to how important  charitable giving is  regardless of your income now a lot of  it goes to religious organizations so we  have a lot of people very passionate  about their religion and supporting the  causes of their church or house of  worship we have a lot of pet lovers you  know a lot goes to the pets a lot goes  to cancer researcher alzheimer's  something that is maybe has personally  affected you so there's a lot of real  passion about where our dollars go and  understanding that the dollars we give  to charity does make a difference and  how do people give uh marcia are they  giving on a monthly basis where it's a  recurring payment or are they doing it  in one lump sum either at the beginning  of the year or at the end of the year  when it perhaps is most  advantageous tax wise or it's just  something that comes up when you're  getting ready to get all your papers in  order  well it's any and all of the above now  the charities will often tell you they  really prefer the recurring you know  five dollars a month ten dollars a month  25 a month whatever you can afford to  give and you have that hit your credit  card  um it works well for your cash flow  management and it works well for the  charity because they now have reliable  amounts that become their sort of their  base that they know is coming in each  month so that's a great way to go but if  you're a procrastinator like so many of  us  here in the middle of december you know  just writing one big check or you know  making one credit card contribution that  works well too so sticking with that i  feel like over the course of the past  two years particularly with a lot of  racial justice causes raising up on  instagram and things like that venmo has  become a really important part of the  charitable giving process um how does  one even begin to sort of keep track of  payments like that and then turn that  into  something that i would involve in my  taxes like i just have no idea where i  would even begin on that process  yeah and i happen to know maddie that  you're a millennial and i'll tell you  the millennial generation has a general  comment they have really embraced this  online giving using venmo or google pay  or whatnot the important thing i think  they're two really big things one is  that you make sure you're giving to an  actual bonafide 501c3  charity  and the other is that it is your  responsibility as the taxpayer you have  to get the documentation  so whether you see something come  through on a statement or whether like  with a credit card you would see that  actual charitable donation on your  credit card statement  or you get it from the charity and you  might have to request it but it's up to  you to track that and make sure you know  if it's five dollars a month to  you know standish cat rescue  make sure you're getting a document that  says you really did make those  contributions  at the same time when people are looking  for organizations i mean you mentioned  everyone has their kind of pet causes  that they want to give to but if you're  looking to broaden your scope and and  affect and  invest in emotionally and financially  more causes how do you go about vetting  different organizations  it's super important that you do that  and there are two organizations that are  kind of the the north stars for the the  charitable giving world  one is charity navigator  and it is charitynavigator.org  they show they show all the details for  any 501c3 organization  so if you just hop online and you can  either google your charity and get to  charity navigator or the other way you  want to look up the the place that you  want to actually give some of your  hard-earned money so make sure it's a  legitimate charity the navigator does a  very good job on a tremendous number  we're talking about somewhere around 1.4  1.5 million charities just in the us  and if you go overseas we've got another  eight or nine million so you do need to  vet the charities and make sure that  it's a legitimate place  and you can get that documentation  the other really good site is  givewell  givewell.org now give well does a really  deep dive into analyses on the charities  not just is it a legitimate charity but  what's its impact and so i talked about  this as a strategy if you really want to  maximize your impact  take a look at the nine or ten charities  that they have vetted to say of all the  ones out there these are the ones by the  numbers that spend the least amount of  money on administrative stuff you know  not on the people  and they maximize the impact that it  actually gives to people and communities  so one that they have it's very highly  rated as one of the malaria you know how  the netting in many of the countries in  africa if we can just put netting around  children's beds it prevents the  mosquitoes in and prevents malaria  or reduces it greatly  that's a huge impact for pennies  that's the kind of thing they're looking  for and it's just so interesting to see  the charities that they're ranking very  high for those reasons maximum impact  marcia really quickly here how do you  decide how much to give based on your  income  not how much it hurts you know you may  have heard that give till it hurts don't  give till it hurts  give an amount that you feel comfortable  with you can always change it next year  or if you can do only one time 25 this  year fantastic they'll appreciate it  because you basically end up pooling  all the money with everyone else who's  giving to that same charity so give what  you can and what you're comfortable with  a great reminder this holiday season of  the importance of giving marcia thank  you so much for being here that was  marcia mantel from mental retirement  consulting joining us in boston

